"""Explicit river-process teaching content. Assembly uses the fixed approved slots."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['A drainage basin is the area drained by a river and its tributaries; its divide separates it from neighbouring basins.','In this NSSCO context watershed names the drainage divide, while catchment names contributing area.'],
'01-01':['A river channel is the depression containing flow, with bed and banks; it can be seasonally dry.','Floodwater extending onto surrounding ground is not the same as the normal channel.'],
'01-02':['Catchment area contributes surface drainage towards a specified common outlet.','A tributary catchment can be nested within the catchment of a larger river, depending on the outlet chosen.'],
'01-03':['A drainage divide commonly follows higher ground separating surface-runoff directions.','Tracing the divide identifies a boundary, whereas shading its enclosed contributing land identifies the basin area.'],
'02':['A typical longitudinal profile is steeper near the source and gentler downstream, with local exceptions.','Channel cross-sections, valley profiles and longitudinal profiles show different dimensions and must not be confused.'],
'02-01':['Typical upland upper reaches have steeper gradients and narrower valleys; vertical erosion and slope processes shape them.','A waterfall can reflect local geological controls and does not by itself prove a reach is upper course.'],
'02-02':['Middle reaches commonly have wider valleys and developing floodplains, with lateral erosion and deposition.','A meandering reach combines outer-bank erosion and inner-bank deposition rather than stopping erosion downstream.'],
'02-03':['Lower reaches commonly have low overall gradients, broader floodplains and larger channels.','Low gradient alone does not prove low velocity, because channel geometry, roughness and discharge matter.'],
'03':['Hydraulic action uses water force; abrasion uses sediment contact with channel surfaces; solution dissolves susceptible minerals.','Attrition wears the load through particle collisions, while traction and suspension describe movement rather than erosion.'],
'03-01':['Water pressure and turbulence can detach material from weaknesses in a channel bank or bed.','An observed water-driven detachment need not involve a stone striking the surface.'],
'03-02':['Corrosion or solution erosion chemically dissolves susceptible material into the water.','Corrosion must be distinguished from corrasion, which is mechanical abrasion.'],
'03-03':['Corrasion or abrasion wears channel surfaces through contact with transported sand and stones.','A particle rolling along the bed describes traction; its scraping of the bed additionally supports abrasion.'],
'03-04':['Attrition is wear and rounding of transported particles as they collide with each other.','A fresh downstream sediment input can introduce angular material despite an overall rounding trend.'],
'04':['Waterfalls, rapids, meanders, potholes and gorges have different forms and formation histories.','A landform explanation needs a supported process sequence rather than an undifferentiated list of erosion names.'],
'04-01':['A resistant cap above weaker rock can be undercut, collapse and retreat upstream as a waterfall develops.','Hydraulic action and abrasion can excavate a plunge pool at the foot of falling water.'],
'04-02':['Rapids involve turbulent flow over an irregular or steeply sloping reach with smaller steps or obstacles.','A series of smaller drops should not be described as a single large vertical waterfall without evidence.'],
'04-03':['In a typical free meander, outer-bank erosion contrasts with inner-bank deposition and a point bar.','A neck cutoff and later reduced connection can leave an oxbow lake, but not every bend inevitably cuts off.'],
'04-04':['Swirling sediment can abrade a rock-bed hollow and enlarge a pothole.','The enlargement of the hollow is abrasion of the bed, whereas collisions wearing the stones are attrition of the load.'],
'04-05':['A gorge is a deep narrow steep-sided valley; waterfall retreat can leave a gorge downstream.','A gorge can also have other incision histories, so its shape alone does not establish waterfall retreat.'],
'05':['River load can move by traction, saltation, suspension and solution transport.','Movement mode depends on flow and particle properties; more than one mode can operate in the same reach.'],
'05-01':['Traction is rolling or sliding particles along the bed.','Bed contact identifies traction more directly than particle size alone.'],
'05-02':['Saltation is repeated short hopping or bouncing near the bed with intermittent bed contact.','One isolated particle position above the bed does not establish its full movement history.'],
'05-03':['Suspension carries particles within the water, supported by turbulent motions under suitable conditions.','Suspended solids can settle when supporting turbulence declines, while dissolved substances do not settle in the same mechanical way.'],
'05-04':['Dissolved substances move with river water as solution transport, including in apparently clear water.','Dissolution that removes rock and subsequent transport of dissolved products are related but distinct stages.'],
'06':['Solid deposition occurs when local conditions no longer sustain transport of supplied particles.','Deposition can be selective: coarse particles stop while finer or dissolved material continues.'],
'06-01':['Discharge is flow volume per time and equals cross-sectional area times mean velocity in a simple channel calculation.','An increase in discharge can occur with lower velocity if cross-sectional area increases sufficiently.'],
'06-02':['Reduced velocity and turbulence can allow some supplied solid sediment to settle or stop moving.','An observed depositional pool can later lose stored material during stronger flow.'],
'06-03':['Load properties and bedrock-controlled channel form influence the locations and sizes of deposited particles.','Resistance of bedrock needs a flow or geometry pathway to explain deposition; hardness alone is not deposition.'],
'07':['Deltas, natural levees, marshes and floodplains are distinct but potentially connected depositional settings.','Low relief alone does not distinguish these features; water pathways, vegetation, origin and setting matter.'],
'07-01':['Deltas accumulate where river sediment is supplied faster than removal at entry into a standing water body.','Distributaries split away across a delta, unlike tributaries joining a main river.'],
'07-02':['Natural levees are raised river-side deposits built by repeated uneven overbank sedimentation.','Natural levees can be overtopped or breached and differ in origin from constructed embankments.'],
'07-03':['Marshes commonly have waterlogged ground and non-woody wetland vegetation, with seasonal or persistent wetness.','A dry-date image does not establish absence of seasonal wetland function.'],
'07-04':['Floodplains are relatively flat river-side surfaces formed and modified by migration, erosion and alluvial deposition.','One recent flood outline is not necessarily the complete landform boundary.'],
'07-05':['Oshanas are shallow seasonal watercourses in the Cuvelai system, connected with pans and seasonal wetlands.','A dry oshana can carry water again; its dry appearance does not prove it is safe to obstruct or occupy.'],
'08':['Wetland resources can support water use, farming, grazing, fishing and habitats, subject to conditions and access.','Flooding and waterlogging can damage activities even where wetland resources provide long-term benefits.'],
'08-01':['Seasonal water and suitable alluvial soils can create livelihood opportunities in some wetland settings.','Benefits differ across users and seasons, so a wetland cannot be assumed uniformly fertile or permanently supplied.'],
'08-02':['Low-lying wetland locations can face inundation, access disruption, waterlogging and water-quality problems.','A decision about location must consider flood timing and exposure as well as access to resources.'],
'09':['Drainage, abstraction, pollution and obstructed flow paths can alter wetland functions through distinct mechanisms.','Wetland management needs a stated objective, suitable action and evidence of its performance.'],
'09-01':['A road with inadequate or blocked openings can obstruct shallow flow and alter local inundation.','An unusually wet year is a possible alternative influence on water level, so before/after comparisons need rainfall context.'],
'09-02':['Suitable crossing openings, pollution controls, buffer protection and informed settlement planning can address specific wetland problems.','Construction or an assigned responsibility is not proof of management success; monitoring must assess the intended result.'],
'91':['A safe channel cross-section records width and depth at specified positions across a line perpendicular to flow.','Repeated comparable measurements and dated records support interpretation, while one reach and date do not describe the whole river.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Name the boundary separating neighbouring drainage basins.','A drainage divide, called a watershed in this NSSCO context.',['1 mark for the name watershed or drainage divide.']),
'002':('Describe the drainage arrangement in the supplied original basin map, using the main channel, tributaries, divide and outlet O.','Tributaries join the main channel upstream of O. The divide encloses the land contributing surface drainage towards O. Adjacent slopes across the divide drain towards a different outlet.',['1 mark for the joining-channel pattern; 1 for the contributing-area relationship supported by the map; 1 for the boundary/opposite-drainage evidence.']),
'003':('Describe the channel evidence in this record: a depression contains seasonal water; P is its bottom, Q its side, and floodwater sometimes spreads beyond Q onto flatter ground.','The depression is the river channel. P identifies its bed and Q a bank. Water beyond Q is overbank flooding, rather than evidence that the whole flooded plain is the normal channel.',['1 mark for identifying the channel feature from the depression; 1 for P/Q bed–bank interpretation using the observation evidence; 1 for the overbank distinction.']),
'004':('Describe the catchment of outlet O from this schematic record: valleys A and B drain into O; valley C drains east to outlet E; twelve full and four half grid squares contribute to O, each 1 km².','The catchment includes land draining through A and B towards O, excluding C. Its estimated area is 14 km². The estimate concerns contributing land, not the length of the channel.',['1 mark for the A/B versus C contributing-area pattern; 1 for the 14 km² estimate using the grid evidence; 1 for identifying area rather than channel length.']),
'005':('Describe the divide evidence: a ridge separates valleys B and C; arrows on the B slope lead towards O and arrows on the C slope lead towards E.','The ridge separates opposing drainage directions. It forms part of the boundary between the O and E catchments. It is a dividing line, not the whole area supplying either outlet.',['1 mark for the opposite-flow relationship; 1 for relating the ridge observation to the catchment boundary; 1 for the boundary–area distinction.']),
'006':('Name the profile that follows a river’s height from source towards mouth.','The longitudinal or long profile.',['1 mark for the name longitudinal/long profile.']),
'007':('Describe the downstream gradient pattern in the supplied longitudinal profile, using its height and distance scales.','The profile falls towards the outlet. Its upper part is steeper and its lower part gentler overall. Local steps should be identified where the actual profile shows them, rather than assuming a perfectly smooth change.',['1 mark for the overall fall; 1 for the changing steepness pattern supported by scaled evidence; 1 for a correctly described local feature or exception visible in the resource.']),
'008':('Describe upper-reach characteristics from these observations: closely spaced valley-side contours, a narrow valley floor, a confined channel and a large height drop over a short distance.','The valley sides are steep and the floor narrow. The channel is confined by those slopes. The large drop over a short distance indicates a steep longitudinal gradient.',['1 mark for steep/narrow valley features; 1 for interpreting channel confinement from the data; 1 for the height–distance gradient relationship.']),
'009':('Describe this middle-reach resource: tributaries join a larger channel, the valley floor is wider than at the upstream site, and bends have outer cliffs opposite inner sand bars.','The valley and channel are larger than upstream in the record. Tributaries add connected drainage routes. The bends show lateral erosion opposite deposition, consistent with meander development.',['1 mark for the wider valley/channel pattern; 1 for the tributary-join evidence; 1 for the outer-cliff/inner-bar interpretation.']),
'010':('Describe the lower-reach interpretation supported by this record: broad low-lying plain, large channel and low height drop over 20 km; velocity was not measured.','The resource shows a broad floodplain setting and low overall gradient. The large channel is consistent with a lower reach. The record does not establish slow velocity because no speed data are supplied.',['1 mark for the broad-low plain feature; 1 for using the low drop over distance as gradient evidence; 1 for the justified limit on velocity inference.']),
'011':('Describe how hydraulic action, abrasion and attrition differ in what acts on what.','Hydraulic action uses water force on channel material. Abrasion uses transported particles scraping or striking channel surfaces. Attrition involves load particles colliding and wearing each other.',['1 mark for the water-force action step; 1 for load-to-channel wear; 1 for load-to-load wear and its particle-changing result.']),
'012':('Describe the erosion mechanisms supported by this record: A = water pressure detaches a jointed block; B = sand scrapes a bed; C = two pebbles collide and lose corners.','A supports hydraulic action. B is abrasion of the channel bed. C is attrition of the load. The defining evidence is the action and contact, not simply that water is present.',['1 mark for A’s water-force feature; 1 for interpreting B’s scraping evidence; 1 for C’s particle-collision and rounding relationship.']),
'013':('Describe hydraulic-action evidence in this observation: repeated surges enter a bank crack, press against it and detach a loose block; no stone impact is recorded.','Water acts directly on the crack and block. Repeated pressure and turbulence are consistent with detachment by hydraulic action. The observation does not require sediment striking the bank to explain that event.',['1 mark for the direct water-force feature; 1 for using crack-pressure/detachment evidence; 1 for distinguishing the supported mechanism from an unobserved sediment impact.']),
'014':('Describe the solution-erosion interpretation: weakly acidic water contacts limestone, analysis finds dissolved carbonate products, and the surface is gradually removed without visible scraping.','The rock is being removed chemically rather than by mechanical scratching. Dissolved carbonate products support dissolution of susceptible limestone. This is corrosion or solution erosion.',['1 mark for the chemical-removal feature; 1 for the dissolved-product evidence; 1 for the corrosion/solution interpretation.']),
'015':('Describe abrasion in this record: sand and pebbles strike a bedrock surface, scratches develop and material is removed from the bed.','The transported load contacts the bed. Its scraping and impacts wear the channel surface. The scratches and removed material support corrasion/abrasion, rather than only movement along the bed.',['1 mark for the load-to-bed relationship; 1 for the wear evidence; 1 for interpreting the process as abrasion rather than transport alone.']),
'016':('Describe the attrition evidence: repeated collisions between transported pebbles break off projecting corners, leaving smaller rounder particles.','The contact is between load particles. Corners and fragments are lost through impacts. The smaller, rounder result supports attrition rather than direct scraping of the channel bed.',['1 mark for the particle-to-particle relationship; 1 for the loss-of-corners observation; 1 for the rounding/attrition interpretation.']),
'017':('Describe the sequence producing a retreating waterfall where resistant rock overlies weaker rock.','Erosion excavates a plunge pool and undercuts weaker rock beneath the cap. The unsupported cap collapses. Repeated erosion and collapse shift the fall upstream, leaving a gorge downstream.',['1 mark for the first pool/undercutting step; 1 for unsupported-cap collapse; 1 for upstream retreat and gorge as the result.']),
'018':('Describe the erosion landforms and process sequence in the supplied river-resource figure, using its waterfall, plunge pool and gorge evidence.','The figure’s abrupt drop identifies the waterfall, with a local hollow at its foot as the plunge pool. Undercutting and cap collapse permit upstream retreat. The narrow steep-sided reach left downstream is the gorge.',['1 mark for the waterfall/pool feature relationship; 1 for interpreting the undercut-collapse evidence; 1 for the retreat/gorge pattern in the figure.']),
'019':('Describe the waterfall-retreat evidence: a plunge pool erodes weaker rock beneath a resistant cap; later drawings place the collapsed lip 12 m upstream of its first position.','The cap was undercut and lost support. Collapse allowed the lip to occupy a position farther upstream. The supplied 12 m displacement records retreat, without establishing a rate unless dates are provided.',['1 mark for the undercut-cap feature; 1 for the upstream positional evidence; 1 for distinguishing retreat distance from an unsupported rate.']),
'020':('Describe the form supported by this resource: a turbulent reach has several small drops over an irregular bed and protruding rocks, rather than one large vertical drop.','The reach contains rapids. Broken flow occurs across multiple small bed steps and obstacles. This pattern differs from a single large waterfall drop.',['1 mark for the rapids feature; 1 for the multiple-step/obstacle evidence; 1 for the form contrast with a waterfall.']),
'021':('Describe the bend pattern using these paired values: outer bank depth 2.4 m and near-bank speed 0.9 m/s; inner bank depth 0.8 m and speed 0.3 m/s, with an exposed sand bar.','The outside is deeper and faster in this record. The inside is shallower and slower, with deposited sand. This supports outer-bank erosion opposite inner-bank deposition in a meander.',['1 mark for the outer depth/speed pattern with evidence; 1 for the inner-bar and slower-flow observations; 1 for the paired erosion/deposition interpretation.']),
'022':('Describe the pothole mechanism supported by this resource: sediment circulates within a rounded bedrock hollow and repeatedly scrapes its floor and walls.','The hollow is a pothole-like feature. Circulating sediment abrades its rock surfaces. Repeated scraping can enlarge the hollow; that bed wear differs from attrition wearing the stones themselves.',['1 mark for the rounded bedrock-hollow feature; 1 for the circulating-sediment evidence; 1 for the abrasion/enlargement interpretation.']),
'023':('Describe the gorge interpretation in this sequence: a waterfall retreats upstream and leaves a long, narrow reach with steep valley sides downstream of its new lip.','The downstream reach has a narrow floor and steep sides, consistent with a gorge. Its position behind the retreating fall links it to the supplied retreat sequence. It is an extended valley, not just the local plunge pool.',['1 mark for the gorge shape pattern; 1 for the position/retreat evidence; 1 for distinguishing the valley reach from the pool.']),
'024':('Describe traction, saltation and suspension as river-transport movements.','Traction rolls or slides particles along the bed. Saltation moves them in repeated short hops with bed contacts. Suspension carries particles within the water for longer intervals under suitable conditions.',['1 mark for the bed-contact traction step; 1 for repeated hopping; 1 for the suspended movement and its different bed-contact result.']),
'025':('Describe the transport modes in this record: A rolls along the bed; B repeatedly hops and lands; C is carried within the water; D moves as dissolved substances.','A is traction, B saltation, C suspension and D solution transport. A and B involve bed contacts, while C remains within the water during the observation and D is dissolved rather than a visible solid grain.',['1 mark for the A/B movement contrast; 1 for the suspension feature using C; 1 for the dissolved-state evidence at D.']),
'026':('Describe the traction evidence: a labelled cobble rolls along the bed during high flow and remains in repeated contact with the surface.','The particle moves along the bed rather than remaining suspended. Rolling and repeated contact identify traction. The observation concerns the stated high-flow interval, not proof that the cobble moves under every condition.',['1 mark for the along-bed pattern; 1 for rolling/contact evidence; 1 for a correctly limited traction interpretation.']),
'027':('Describe the saltation evidence: a time sequence shows a grain briefly rising, following a short arc and landing on the bed repeatedly.','The grain makes repeated short hops. Its bed contacts separate the hops. That movement pattern supports saltation, rather than continuous rolling or long-duration suspension.',['1 mark for the short-hop feature; 1 for the repeated landing evidence; 1 for distinguishing the movement from the alternatives.']),
'028':('Describe the suspension evidence: fine solid particles travel within turbulent water and later settle when the sample is left still.','The solids are carried within the water during flow. Turbulence supports their suspended movement. Settling after the water becomes still is consistent with reduced support, rather than the particles having been dissolved.',['1 mark for the within-water feature; 1 for the turbulence evidence; 1 for interpreting later settling without confusing solution.']),
'029':('Describe the dissolved-load interpretation: a clear river-water sample contains dissolved calcium compounds that pass with water through the stated fine filter; no visible solid grains are recorded.','Clear appearance does not mean no load. The measured compounds are dissolved and move with the water as solution transport. They are not solid grains settling mechanically when velocity decreases.',['1 mark for the clear-water/load relationship; 1 for using the dissolved-compound evidence; 1 for distinguishing solution transport from solid settling.']),
'030':('Explain how reduced local velocity can cause selective sediment deposition when gravel and fine suspended particles enter a pool.','Lower velocity and turbulence reduce the ability to keep some particles moving. Gravel can stop near the pool entrance, while some finer material remains supported. Deposition is selective because particle properties and flow conditions differ.',['1 mark for reduced velocity as the causal factor; 1 for the reduced-transport link; 1 for coarse accumulation as an effect; 1 for explaining why finer material may continue rather than all load settling.']),
'031':('Describe the deposition pattern: a stream entering a wider pool slows from 0.9 to 0.3 m/s; gravel accumulates near the entrance, some sand farther in, and fine material leaves in the outflow.','Velocity is lower within the pool. Coarse gravel settles earlier than some sand, while fine material continues downstream. The pattern supports selective deposition as transporting conditions decline.',['1 mark for the lower-velocity pattern with values; 1 for the spatial grain-size evidence; 1 for the selective-deposition interpretation.']),
'032':('Name the quantity measured in cubic metres per second that records river-flow volume passing a cross-section per time.','Discharge.',['1 mark for the name discharge.']),
'033':('Name the flow quantity measured in metres per second that can decrease locally as a river enters a depositional pool.','Velocity or speed.',['1 mark for the name velocity/speed.']),
'034':('Name one load property, besides flow velocity, that influences whether a solid particle is deposited.','Particle size; density or shape is also valid.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant load property; accept size, density or shape.']),
'035':('Name the raised natural deposit built beside a river channel by repeated uneven overbank sedimentation.','A natural levee.',['1 mark for the name natural levee.']),
'036':('Describe the landform contrasts in this resource: A branches across a deposit into a lake; B is a raised river-side sediment ridge; C is a broad flat alluvial surface beside a meandering river.','A is consistent with a delta and distributaries. B is a natural levee if its stated origin is flood deposition. C is a floodplain. Their water pathways and forms distinguish them despite shared deposition.',['1 mark for A’s mouth/branching feature; 1 for B’s ridge and origin evidence; 1 for C’s broad river-side plain interpretation.']),
'037':('Describe the delta evidence: a river enters a lake, divides into three outward-flowing branches and crosses a sediment-built area extending into the lake.','The deposit lies at the river–lake entry. Channels split into distributaries across it. The extending sediment-built pattern supports a delta, rather than merely a tributary junction.',['1 mark for the entry/deposit position feature; 1 for the outward-branching evidence; 1 for the delta interpretation and distinction from joining tributaries.']),
'038':('Describe the natural-levee pattern: sediment beside the channel is coarser and 12 cm thick after a flood; a deposit farther across the plain is finer and 3 cm thick.','The thicker coarse deposit is concentrated beside the channel. Finer thinner material lies farther away. This supports uneven overbank deposition that can build a natural levee through repeated events, without proving its complete history from one flood.',['1 mark for the near-channel coarse/thick feature with evidence; 1 for the farther fine/thin contrast; 1 for a qualified levee-development interpretation.']),
'039':('Describe the marsh interpretation: the record shows shallow waterlogged ground with extensive reeds on low land away from the main channel, with seasonally changing water cover.','The low area has waterlogged conditions and non-woody wetland vegetation. Reeds and shallow wetness support a marsh interpretation. Seasonal water-cover change means one dry image would not establish absence of wetland function.',['1 mark for the wet-ground feature; 1 for the reed/non-woody vegetation evidence; 1 for the seasonal interpretation.']),
'040':('Describe the floodplain evidence: a wide low-gradient valley floor lies beside a meandering river, with alluvial layers and several abandoned channels.','The broad low river-side surface is consistent with a floodplain. Alluvium supports deposition, while abandoned channels indicate migration. Both processes help explain the surface rather than one recent flood alone.',['1 mark for the broad-low river-side feature; 1 for alluvial evidence; 1 for interpreting abandoned-channel migration in the landform history.']),
'041':('Describe the oshana setting in this resource: shallow seasonal channels link depressions across an inland plain; some are dry in the photographed month but local records show later inundation.','The drainage is shallow and seasonally connected across low relief. Dry conditions on one date do not mean the channels are inactive permanently. The later inundation evidence supports retaining the seasonal-flow interpretation.',['1 mark for the shallow connected-channel pattern; 1 for the dry-date evidence; 1 for the seasonality interpretation using the later record.']),
'042':('Explain one wetland opportunity and one related constraint for farming on a floodplain.','Water and suitable deposited soils can support crops. Yet seasonal inundation can waterlog or destroy a standing crop and delay access. The benefit depends on timing, crop choice and the actual soil and water conditions.',['1 mark for an opportunity factor; 1 for the link to crop production; 1 for a flood/waterlogging constraint; 1 for explaining its crop/access effect under stated conditions.']),
'043':('Describe the trade-off in this record: lower field A has easy wet-season water access but frequent inundation; raised field B floods less in the record but needs water transport during dry conditions.','A combines water access with greater recorded flood exposure. B has less recorded inundation but a water-access cost. The pattern does not establish one universally superior field without the intended crop and timing.',['1 mark for A’s paired benefit/risk pattern using the evidence; 1 for B’s water-access trade-off using the record; 1 for a conditional interpretation rather than an unsupported universal choice.']),
'044':('Name one livelihood that seasonal wetland water can support.','Fishing; appropriate water use, cultivation or grazing is also acceptable.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant livelihood or resource use.']),
'045':('Name one wetland constraint that can prevent cultivation even after visible floodwater recedes.','Waterlogging.',['1 mark for the name waterlogging.']),
'046':('Explain how a road crossing with blocked openings can affect shallow wetland flow and how a targeted response could help.','Blocked openings obstruct water movement, which can cause upstream ponding or redirected flow. Assessing and clearing suitable openings can restore connectivity, provided their capacity and position suit the flow. Performance needs checking during relevant conditions.',['1 mark for obstruction as a cause; 1 for the flow-to-ponding/redirected-water link; 1 for clearing/assessing openings as a targeted response; 1 for the conditional connectivity effect and need to check performance.']),
'047':('Describe what this wetland record supports: upstream water was higher after road raising; the culvert is blocked; that year also had unusually heavy rainfall.','Higher upstream water is associated with a documented obstruction. The blocked culvert provides a plausible flow pathway for the effect. Heavy rainfall is an additional influence, so the record does not isolate the road as the sole cause.',['1 mark for the upstream-water pattern; 1 for using culvert evidence to support a mechanism; 1 for the rainfall limitation on causal interpretation.']),
'048':('Name one human action that can degrade wetland water quality through a direct pollutant pathway.','Discharging untreated wastewater into a connected channel.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant polluting action with a water pathway.']),
'049':('Name one management measure that directly addresses a blocked wetland crossing.','Assess and clear or repair the crossing’s culverts to maintain suitable flow openings.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant crossing/culvert measure.']),
'050':('Describe a safe method for recording a channel cross-section at an approved shallow site.','Stretch a tape perpendicular to flow to record width. Measure vertical depths at stated regular positions across it using consistent units. Record each position and depth, using supplied data instead if conditions are unsafe.',['1 mark for the first perpendicular-width step; 1 for consistent vertical depth measurements at stated positions; 1 for the recorded position–depth result with safe conditions retained.']),
'051':('Explain why a channel-width comparison needs similar water-level conditions or a clear record of differences between observation dates.','Water level can change the measured width at one site. Without comparable conditions or a dated level record, an apparent spatial difference could reflect timing rather than a persistent channel difference.',['1 mark for the reason linking water level to measured width; 1 for explaining the resulting risk of a misleading site comparison.']),
'052':('Explain the error in using the same shaded area for a drainage basin and its watershed. In a teaching map, A marks contributing slopes and B traces the enclosing ridge.','The confusion is between an area and its boundary. A identifies the catchment, while B traces the divide or watershed. The test is whether the marking fills contributing land or traces its separating edge.',['1 mark for the area/boundary confusion; 1 for the correct A/B distinction and discriminating test.']),
'053':('Explain why “rolling stones are traction, so traction is the erosion of the channel” confuses two ideas. The record separately shows the stones scraping the bed.','Traction describes movement along the bed. The scraping that wears the channel is abrasion. The correct test distinguishes how the stone travels from which surface is worn and by what contact.',['1 mark for identifying the transport/erosion confusion; 1 for the correct traction–abrasion distinction applied through the test of movement versus wear.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','03','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the boundary separating neighbouring river basins. [1]\n(b) Describe hydraulic action, abrasion and attrition by identifying the main contact in each. [3]\n(c) Name the transport mode in which particles hop near the bed. [1]\n(d) Describe two movement contrasts in this resource: A rolls while touching the bed; B remains as fine solid particles within the flowing water. [2]','answer':'(a) Drainage divide/watershed. (b) Hydraulic action is water force on channel material; abrasion is load contacting and wearing the channel; attrition is particles colliding and wearing one another. (c) Saltation. (d) A is along-bed traction with contact; B is suspension within the water without continuing bed contact during the observation.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for the boundary name as the required point.','(b) 3 A marks for the three distinct action/contact descriptions; maximum one per process.','(c) 1 A mark for saltation.','(d) 2 B marks for interpreting the given movement points: along-bed rolling/contact at A and within-water suspended particles at B.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['04-03','06','07-02'],'prompt':'An original river resource records: outer bend depth 2.4 m and speed 0.9 m/s; inner bend depth 0.8 m and speed 0.3 m/s with an exposed sand bar. A nearby flood deposit is 12 cm thick and coarse beside the channel, but 3 cm and finer farther away.\n(a) Name the bend landform. [1]\n(b) Describe the bend’s paired depth/speed pattern and process interpretation. [3]\n(c) Describe how the flood-deposit pattern supports natural-levee development. [2]\n(d) What judgement is supported about these observations proving a pattern in every season? [1]','answer':'(a) Meander. (b) The outer bank is deeper and faster using 2.4 m/0.9 m/s against 0.8 m/0.3 m/s; the inner side has a sand bar, supporting outer erosion opposite inner deposition. (c) Greater coarse near-channel accumulation contrasts with a thinner finer deposit farther away; repeated such events can build a river-side levee. (d) No: one set of observations does not establish all-season conditions.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for meander.','(b) 3 B marks for the depth comparison, speed comparison and process interpretation using the sand-bar evidence; retain units.','(c) 2 B marks for the coarse/thick versus fine/thin data contrast and its qualified connection to repeated levee building.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported decision that the observation window cannot prove every-season conditions.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Describe',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['07-05','08','09'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that a dry oshana is a suitable place to build because there is no water visible today? In a hypothetical Cuvelai planning exercise, the marked shallow channel is dry in the current image, but dated local records show it carrying water in several wet seasons. A proposed road has no openings across that channel; an alternative crossing includes assessed culverts and a maintenance plan. Judge flood exposure and flow connectivity, not present-day appearance alone. [5]','answer':'Disagree with the claim. Dry appearance on one date does not remove the documented seasonal channel function. Building in the flow path creates exposure when water returns, and a road without openings could obstruct or redirect it. The alternative culvert crossing addresses connectivity more directly if its design is suitable and maintenance occurs. Seasonal records and actual flow paths should therefore guide siting; neither dry appearance nor a plan on paper proves safety.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judging the supplied dry-date/seasonal-record evidence contrast, exposure within a flow path, obstruction by the road and the conditional effectiveness of suitable maintained openings. Develop the relevant links; do not credit unrelated wetland lists.','1 C mark for a supported overall judgement against the stated flood/connectivity criterion.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
